    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0032" n="26" xml:id="pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/18467: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-13T15:00:00+03:00">January 13, 1942—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:01 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>43. Inquired of Vyshinski<anchor n="14" xml:id="d23-f1"/> last night with respect
                    to rumors (reported to the Embassy by both the AP<anchor n="15" xml:id="d23-f2"
                    /> and UP<anchor n="16" xml:id="d23-f3"/> representatives) to the effect that
                    conversations are in progress between the Soviet and Finnish Governments.</p>
                <p>Vyshinski replied that he had not heard of the matter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d23-f1" n="14" xml:id="d23-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs
                    of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f2" n="15" xml:id="d23-f2-content">
                <p>Henry Cassidy was Associated Press correspondent in the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f3" n="16" xml:id="d23-f3-content">
                <p>Henry Shapiro was United Press correspondent in the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
